Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = stylized children's picture-book illustration; painted storybook look, soft watercolor edges, warm nursery light, velveteen textures.

FADE IN:

EXT. NURSERY WINDOW - CHRISTMAS DAWN

Frost laces the glass. Inside, a child's stocking bulges on the bedpost.

(SOUND: distant church bells, soft crackle of a hearth)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

INT. BOY'S NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A fat, bunchy VELVETEEN RABBIT sits wedged in the top of a stocking—spotted brown-and-white coat, real thread whiskers, ears lined with pink sateen, a sprig of holly between his paws.

(SOUND: tissue paper rustling, children's delighted gasps)

Nuts, oranges, a toy engine, and a clockwork mouse tumble out around him. THE BOY, about seven, freckled, in a nightshirt, hugs the Rabbit tight for a long moment.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

Aunts and Uncles fill the doorway with parcels. In the rush of new presents, the Rabbit is set aside on the nursery floor.

(SOUND: laughter, paper tearing, footsteps fading)

INT. NURSERY - DAY

The toy cupboard stands open. Expensive mechanical toys gleam on the shelf. The MODEL BOAT, paint worn, sits stiff. TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden lion, holds his chin high. The Velveteen Rabbit lies alone near the fender, shy and still.

(SOUND: clock ticking, faint whir of clockwork)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a long time he lived in the toy cupboard or on the nursery floor, and no one thought very much about him.

The mechanical toys turn their painted faces away. The Skin Horse—ancient, brown coat bald in patches, seams showing, tail hairs sparse—lies close beside the Rabbit.

SKIN HORSE
(gentle)
They come and go. Mainsprings break. They never turn into anything else.

The Rabbit's thread whiskers tremble.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you.

(SOUND: coals settling in the grate)

SKIN HORSE
When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

The Skin Horse's worn eye catches the light.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby.

(SOUND: soft nursery hush, wind at the window)

SKIN HORSE
But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse only smiles.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago. Once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs, longing and a little afraid.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA, stout, apron crisp, grey hair pinned, swoops about like a wind, hustling toys into cupboards.

(SOUND: cupboard doors thudding, skirts swishing)

THE BOY sits up in bed, fretting.

THE BOY
I can't find the china dog.

NANA
Here—take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She drags the Rabbit out by one ear and pushes him into the Boy's arms. The Boy clasps him close.

(SOUND: bedsprings creak, night-light hiss)

INT. BOY'S BED - NIGHT

Moonlight through the curtains. The Boy hugs the Rabbit very tight, rolls over him, pushes him under the pillow. The Rabbit's pink sateen ears flatten.

(SOUND: slow breathing, distant house settling)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At first he found it rather uncomfortable. But very soon he grew to like it.

The Boy whispers, making tunnels under the bedclothes.

THE BOY
Burrows. Like real rabbits live in.

When the Boy sleeps, the Rabbit snuggles under the warm chin, the Boy's hands clasped round him.

(MUSIC: soft lullaby theme, sparse piano)

INT. BOY'S BED - NIGHT (LATER)

Time passes in the same bed. The Rabbit's velveteen fur grows shabbier. His tail hangs unsewn. Pink rubs off his nose where the Boy has kissed him.

(SOUND: night-light flicker, soft snore)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was so happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring light. The Boy trundles the Rabbit in a wheelbarrow, builds fairy huts under raspberry canes, digs burrows in the flower bed. The Rabbit comes up earthy and damp with dew.

(SOUND: birdsong, wheelbarrow squeak, bees)

NANA appears at dusk with a candle, grumbling as she rubs him with her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Boy sits up in bed, arms stretched out. Nana holds the earth-stained Rabbit.

(SOUND: candle flame flutter)

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Rabbit's boot-button eyes catch a look of wisdom. Nana pauses, studying him.

NANA
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The nursery magic had happened to him, and he was a toy no longer. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

(MUSIC: warm resolving chord)

EXT. WOOD - JUNE EVENING

Long light through bracken. The Boy settles the Rabbit in a little nest among the ferns, then wanders off among the trees.

(SOUND: leaves rustle, ants in dry grass, distant boyish shout)

Two WILD RABBITS creep from the tall bracken—furry, brand-new, seams invisible, bodies changing shape as they move. Their noses twitch. They pad close.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

He hops sideways and stands on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Velveteen Rabbit freezes. His back is all one piece, like a pincushion.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

The Wild Rabbit stretches his neck and looks.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

(SOUND: sharp rabbit laugh, bracken shaking)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

THISTLE
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

He whirls and dances until the Velveteen Rabbit grows dizzy.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

The first Wild Rabbit comes so close his whiskers brush the Velveteen ear—then wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, jumps back.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

(SOUND: running footsteps)

The Boy races past. White tails flash. The wild rabbits vanish into the bracken.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only ants run to and fro. The bracken sways. The sun sinks. Little white moths flutter out. The Boy returns and carries him home.

(SOUND: moth wings, evening wind)

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Weeks later. The Rabbit is very old and shabby—whiskers loved off, ear linings grey, brown spots faded, shape nearly gone. The Boy still holds him as beautiful.

(SOUND: labored breathing, restless sheets)

The Boy's face is flushed. He talks in his sleep. His body burns against the Rabbit. Strange people come and go. A light burns all night. The Rabbit lies hidden under the bedclothes, never stirring.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was afraid that if they found him some one might take him away, and he knew that the Boy needed him.

He creeps to the pillow and whispers plans of gardens and raspberry thickets. The fever turns. The Boy sits up with picture-books, the Rabbit cuddled at his side.

INT. NURSERY - BRIGHT MORNING

Windows stand wide open. The Boy, wrapped in a shawl, has been carried to the balcony. The Rabbit peeps from the bedclothes.

(SOUND: bright birds outside, low adult voices)

NANA and the DOCTOR, black bag in hand, stand by the stripped bed.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs!—Burn it at once. Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

The Rabbit's head sinks into the sheets.

(SOUND: sack cloth dragged, books thudding in)

EXT. GARDEN BY THE FOWL-HOUSE - DUSK

A sack of old picture-books and rubbish sits in the corner behind the fowl-house. The gardener's tools lean idle by the potato rows.

(SOUND: hens settling, evening insects)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The gardener was too busy just then. Next morning he promised to come quite early and burn the whole lot.

INT. DIFFERENT BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Boy sleeps with a new white-plush bunny, glass eyes shining. He smiles, dreaming of waves.

(SOUND: quiet sleep breathing)

EXT. GARDEN BY THE FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

The sack lies untied. The Velveteen Rabbit wriggles his head through the opening. His coat is thin and threadbare. He shivers. Raspberry canes stand tall and close like a jungle.

(SOUND: night wind in canes, distant owl)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought of those long sunlit hours in the garden—how happy they were—and a great sadness came over him.

Memory light flickers in his boot-button eyes: fairy huts, ants on his paws, the day he knew he was Real, the wise Skin Horse.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
(soft, almost to himself)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear trickles down his shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

(SOUND: single soft drop on earth)

Where the tear falls, a mysterious flower pushes up—slender emerald leaves, a blossom like a golden cup. It opens. Out steps the NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY—dress of pearl and dew-drops, flowers at her neck and in her hair, face like the most perfect flower.

(MUSIC: delicate chime theme, rising)

She gathers him up and kisses his damp velveteen nose.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I—I think I have seen you.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved.

She holds him closer.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

(SOUND: soft rush of air, leaves answering)

EXT. WOOD GLADE - MOONLIGHT

The Fairy flies with him into the wood. Bracken fronds shine like frosted silver. In the open glade, wild rabbits dance with their shadows on the velvet grass—then stop and stand in a ring, staring.

(SOUND: padded feet on grass, night birds)

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the little Rabbit again and sets him on the grass.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

He sits quite still, remembering his missing hind legs—too shy to move. Something tickles his nose. Without thinking he lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

(SOUND: sudden scratch, then a gasp of wonder)

He has hind legs. Brown fur, soft and shiny, instead of dingy velveteen. Ears twitch by themselves. Long whiskers brush the grass.

He gives one leap—then springs about the turf, jumping sideways, whirling as the others do, wild with joy.

(MUSIC: bright leaping theme)

When at last he stops to look for the Fairy, she is gone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - SPRING DAY

Months later. Warm sun. The Boy, taller by a season, plays among the trees. Two rabbits creep from the bracken and peep at him.

(SOUND: breeze in new leaves, distant laughter)

One is brown all over. The other has strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted, and the spots still show through. About his soft nose and round black eyes there is something familiar.

THE BOY
(quiet, to himself)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The spotted rabbit watches the child who first helped him to be Real. Then both rabbits slip back into the bracken.

(SOUND: soft hop through ferns, wind easing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

The wood holds its green light. A last freckle of sun rests on the empty path.

(MUSIC: gentle closing lullaby, fading)

FADE OUT.

THE END
